Correction to this commit message.

The opening paragraph says the one-time code "meant waiting for an SMS, which
costs money per message". That is wrong on both counts, and the code this
commit adds never behaved that way.

Codes are delivered over WhatsApp through the OpenWA gateway.
OtpDeliveryChannel offers only WhatsApp and a development-log channel; there is
no SMS path anywhere in the van sales customer sign-in.
VanSalesCustomerAuthSettings records why WhatsApp was chosen, and one of the
stated reasons is that it costs nothing per message: these customers were
already sending their orders that way, so the number is known to work and the
message arrives where they are already looking.

What the paragraph should have said is that a code has to arrive before anyone
can sign in, and it fails wherever the signal does. That reason stands on its
own and is the real motivation for adding a password.

The rest of the message is accurate: the nullable columns and why, the hashing
and its work factor, the refusals that are indistinguishable in wording and in
time, the shared lockout budget, and the onboarding rules.
